## The loop, in one picture

```
PRD (AC-001...)  ->  acceptance.json (checks)  ->  code + tests
        \                                              |
         \--------------- ./scripts/sdlc loop ---------+
              gates: <%stages%>
              fail -> evidence + heal prompt -> agent/you fix the CODE -> verify again
              stops: green | <%maxIterations%> iterations | <%noProgressLimit%> identical failures | spec/gate files tampered with
```

- **Gates** (lint, types, unit tests with coverage floors, build, API-contract drift, security) run first.
- **Facts** then start the real system and check every acceptance criterion in the PRD against it. A criterion without a check fails verification: *an unverified requirement is not done*.
- Every run writes evidence (logs, `results.json`, `report.md`) to `.ai/evidence/<run>/`; `latest/` is the newest. CI uploads it as an artifact and in the job summary.
- Heal prompts go to the command in `.ai/sdlc.json` -> `agent.command` (any CLI that reads stdin), or to you if it is empty.

Details: [`AGENTS.md`](AGENTS.md) and [`.ai/README.md`](.ai/README.md).

## Add a feature

```bash
./scripts/sdlc new user-login          # creates .ai/specs/FEAT-001-user-login/{prd.md,acceptance.json}
# you write the PRD and the checks (skills: .ai/skills/write-prd, write-fact-checks); that is the only manual part. Then:
./scripts/sdlc implement FEAT-001      # the agent writes tests, code, dependencies and config; the loop proves it
```

`implement` checks the spec is complete, installs the toolchain if needed, runs a baseline, gives the agent your PRD and checks as its prompt,
installs any dependency the agent adds, then verifies and heals until green. It never commits or pushes: you review `git diff`.
The agent is `<%agentPreset%>`<%#agentConfigured%> (`<%agentCommand%>`)<%/agentConfigured%>; change it in `scaffolder.config.yaml` or per run with `SDLC_AGENT_CMD`.
Add `--dry-run` to see the exact prompt and command without running anything. `./scripts/sdlc feature FEAT-001` does the same and also ships (`--deploy <env>`).


